<.shell title="Values" current={:values}>
  <h1>Values</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every date, time, number, and quantity people read comes from one of these functions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each example runs the app's own function, so this page shows what people see. Noon on
    Oct 20, 2026, in Vancouver is "now". The functions are in <code>Service.Format</code>
    unless named.
  </p>

  <section :for={{section, about, rows} <- values()} class="mt-8">
    <h2>{section}</h2>
    <p>{about}</p>
    <.table
      id={"values-#{section |> String.downcase() |> String.replace(" ", "-")}"}
      rows={rows}
      row_id={fn {name, _use, _examples} -> "value-#{name}" end}
    >
      <:col :let={{name, _use, _examples}} label="Function" class="w-px mono whitespace-nowrap">
        {name}
      </:col>
      <:col :let={{_name, use, _examples}} label="Use">{use}</:col>
      <:col :let={{_name, _use, examples}} label="Examples">
        <span :for={{call, result} <- examples} class="block">
          <code>{call}</code> → {result}
        </span>
      </:col>
    </.table>
  </section>

  <h2>Rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Never write a number and its unit by hand, such as <code>&#123;days&#125; days</code>. A test fails on it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Work the number out in <code>Service.Convert</code>, such as <code>days_until/2</code>,
      which rounds up. Format it here.
    </li>
    <li>
      No function fits? Add one to <code>Service.Format</code>, with a test, and a row here.
    </li>
    <li>
      The words follow the <a href={page_path(:writing)}>writing guide</a>: digits, month first, and
      the 24-hour clock.
    </li>
  </ul>
</.shell>
